            <ArticleTitle>[Prevalence of early and late dumping after gastric bypass].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>696-8, 700</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Gastric bypass surgery is an effective treatment of obesity, bringing a significant weight loss and a major improvement of carbohydrate profile. However, in some patients, a deregulation in carbohydrate metabolism between insulin secretion and sensitivity is observed, whereupon early and late dumping happen. Their prevalence isn't well studied, although it seems that 10 to 20% of patients are affected. We've studied a cohort of 70 patients who undergone gastric bypass surgery at the CHUV. 18 (25.7%) patients have a positive anamnesis for early dumping and 10 (14.3%) for late dumping, being it superior as what is described in the literature.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
